@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{org-linear.el} keeps the issues of one or more @uref{https://linear.app, Linear} teams in an Org file and syncs the two in both directions. You work on your issues with the Org commands you already use: change a TODO keyword to move an issue through its workflow, set a deadline for its due date, add a note to comment on it, refile it to move it to another project. Changes made in Linear, by you or by your colleagues, reach the file at the next sync.

Linear stays the authoritative copy and the file is a mirror. The package exists because the alternatives each give up half of that: Linear's own interface lives outside Emacs, and a one-way export cannot be edited. Unlike an export, @samp{org-linear} remembers what each issue looked like at the last sync, so it can tell which side changed a field and merge the two without overwriting either.

The package talks to Linear's GraphQL API through @samp{curl} and converts descriptions and comments between Markdown and Org with @uref{https://pandoc.org, pandoc}. Both run as subprocesses, so Emacs never waits on the network.

Its capabilities fall into four groups:

@itemize
@item
The file: how issues, projects and their fields appear in Org (@ref{The file}).
@item
Syncing: when a sync runs, how it merges, and what happens on conflicts (@ref{Syncing}).
@item
Setup: the API key, the teams and the mappings (@ref{User options}).
@item
The agenda: a single command that lists your open issues, and your deadlines among your other tasks (@ref{The agenda}).
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@samp{org-linear} requires Emacs 29.1 or later, Org 9.7 or later, and the @samp{curl} and @samp{pandoc} programs on your @samp{exec-path}.

With Elpaca:

@lisp
(use-package org-linear
  :ensure (:host github :repo "benthamite/org-linear")
  :custom
  (org-linear-teams '("ENG"))
  :config
  (org-linear-auto-sync-mode))
@end lisp

With Emacs's built-in @samp{package-vc} (Emacs 30 or later):

@lisp
(use-package org-linear
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/benthamite/org-linear")
  :custom
  (org-linear-teams '("ENG"))
  :config
  (org-linear-auto-sync-mode))
@end lisp

Then create a personal API key in Linear (Settings, Security & access, Personal API keys), give it access to the teams you mirror, and make it available to the package (@ref{The API key}).

@node The file
@chapter The file

The file is @code{org-linear-file}. When it does not exist, the first sync creates it with @code{org-linear-file-header} and fills it. Each project is a level-1 heading with a @samp{LINEAR_PROJECT} property, and each issue a level-2 heading under its project. Issues without a project go under a heading named ``No project''.

@example
* Website
:PROPERTIES:
:LINEAR_PROJECT: 3f1c...
:END:
** DOING [#B] Fix the broken signup form                  :ana:bug:
DEADLINE: <2026-11-12 Thu>
:PROPERTIES:
:LINEAR_ID: ENG-123
:LINEAR_UUID: 6b0e...
:LINEAR_URL: https://linear.app/acme/issue/ENG-123/fix-the-broken-signup-form
:END:
:LOGBOOK:
- Note taken on [2026-11-02 Mon 10:14] (Sam Lee) \\
  I can reproduce it on Safari only.
:END:
The form rejects valid email addresses with a plus sign.
@end example

