@node How fields map
@section How fields map

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Linear
@tab Org
@item Title
@tab The heading's title
@item Workflow state
@tab TODO keyword (@ref{Workflow states and keywords})
@item Priority
@tab Priority cookie (@code{org-linear-priorities})
@item Assignee
@tab A tag for the person (@code{org-linear-user-tags})
@item Labels
@tab Tags
@item Due date
@tab @samp{DEADLINE}
@item Project
@tab The level-1 heading the issue sits under
@item Description
@tab The heading's body, converted from and to Markdown by pandoc
@item Comments
@tab Notes in the log drawer
@end multitable

The identifier, the issue's internal ID and its URL are kept in the @samp{LINEAR_ID}, @samp{LINEAR_UUID} and @samp{LINEAR_URL} properties. The sync maintains them; do not edit them.

Tags that name neither a team member nor a label are yours: the sync leaves them alone and never sends them. A label's tag is its name with every character Org does not allow in tags replaced by @samp{_}, so the label @samp{requested-by:peter} becomes the tag @samp{requested_by_peter}.

Linear issues have no start date, so by default @samp{SCHEDULED} is not synced: use it freely to defer an issue in your own agenda.

@vindex org-linear-start-date-label
A team can stand in for a start date with a @samp{Start: YYYY-MM-DD} first line in the description and a label. Set @code{org-linear-start-date-label} to that label's name, such as @samp{"deferred"}, and @samp{SCHEDULED} syncs both ways with the two of them. A date in the file then shows as @samp{SCHEDULED} alone: the line leaves the body, and the label's tag leaves the tags. Setting, moving or removing @samp{SCHEDULED} rewrites the line and sets or removes the label on Linear, and for an issue in triage it also snoozes the issue until that date, or ends the snooze. Nothing is written when you turn the option on, unless a synced heading already has a @samp{SCHEDULED} of its own: that date is sent.

Every @samp{SCHEDULED} on a synced issue is then visible to your team, and the label stays after the date passes, until someone removes the date. A Linear view that hides the label keeps hiding the issue until then.

@node Workflow states and keywords
@section Workflow states and keywords

Each issue's workflow state becomes a TODO keyword. By default the keyword depends only on the state's type (@code{org-linear-type-keywords}): triage issues are @samp{TRIAGE}, backlog issues @samp{BACKLOG}, unstarted ones @samp{TODO}, started ones @samp{DOING}, completed ones @samp{DONE}, and canceled or duplicate ones @samp{CANCELLED}. @code{org-linear-state-keywords} gives individual states keywords of their own, for instance a @samp{WAITING} keyword for a state named ``Waiting''.

When you change a keyword in the file, the sync sends it as the state @code{org-linear-keyword-states} names for it, or else as the first state of the issue's team that maps to that keyword, in Linear's order of state types and positions.

The file's own @samp{#+TODO} line, written from @code{org-linear-file-header} when the file is created, must list every keyword these options can produce. The default header marks @samp{CANCELLED} with @samp{@@}, so Org asks for a note when you cancel an issue; the sync posts that note as a comment, which records why the issue was dropped.

@node Working with issues
@section Working with issues

Edit issues with ordinary Org commands, then save the file; with @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode} the save sends your changes (@ref{Syncing}).

@itemize
@item
To comment on an issue, add a note with @samp{C-c C-z} (@code{org-add-note}). Notes attached to state changes, such as the one the default header asks for when you cancel an issue, are posted too, prefixed with the new keyword.
@item
To create an issue, add a level-2 heading with a TODO keyword under a project heading, with its description as the body. At the next sync it is created in Linear, in the project's team, and gets its @samp{LINEAR_ID}.
@item
To move an issue to another project, refile it under that project's heading.
@item
To assign an issue, tag it with the person's tag; an issue has one assignee, so a heading with two people's tags sends no assignee and the sync log says why.
@end itemize

Deleting a heading does not delete the issue: it comes back at the next sync, with its description and comments. To drop an issue, cancel it.

@findex org-linear-browse
@samp{M-x org-linear-browse} opens the issue at point in your browser.

@findex org-linear-visit
@samp{M-x org-linear-visit} visits @code{org-linear-file}.

@node Closed issues
@section Closed issues

The file holds every open issue of the teams and the issues closed in the last @code{org-linear-closed-retention-days} days. Older closed issues leave the file at the next sync, unless their heading holds something not yet in Linear: unsent edits or notes, or subheadings of your own. Such a heading stays, keeps syncing its edits, and leaves once it has nothing unsent; subheadings keep it for good, and the sync log says so.

@node Syncing
@chapter Syncing

@findex org-linear-sync
@samp{M-x org-linear-sync} saves the file, fetches the teams' issues, merges them with the file and sends the file's changes to Linear. It returns at once; the work runs in the background and a message reports when it starts and how it ended. A background sync reports only changes, so it stays out of your echo area. The first sync converts every description and comment, so it can take a few minutes; later ones convert only what changed.

The merge is three-way. For each field the sync compares the file and Linear with the issue as it was at the last sync, kept in @code{org-linear-state-file}:

@itemize
@item
A field changed only in Linear is updated in the file.
@item
A field changed only in the file is sent to Linear.
@item
When both sides changed a field, Linear's value wins, and yours is recorded in the sync log.
@end itemize

Before each write the sync checks when the issue was last edited. If someone changed it in Linear after the sync read it, the write is refused and the issue is merged again at the next sync, so a change made in Linear is never overwritten. A write Linear rejects, for instance for an invalid value, is logged and not retried until you change what was sent; the sync's other writes still go through.

A sync never changes the file while it has unsaved edits, and never applies changes if the file changed on disk since you visited it. The changes a sync makes clear the buffer's undo history, since undoing them would send a reversal of your colleagues' changes to Linear.

@findex org-linear-auto-sync-mode
@findex org-linear-file-mode
@code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode} is a global minor mode that syncs whenever you save or visit the file, and every @code{org-linear-pull-interval} seconds while the file has no unsaved edits. In the file's buffer it turns on @code{org-linear-file-mode}, which runs a sync after each save.

@findex org-linear-show-log
@samp{M-x org-linear-show-log} shows the sync log: conflicts, refused and rejected writes, issues not created, and errors. When a sync fails, its message points there.

@node The agenda
@chapter The agenda

@findex org-linear-my-issues
@vindex org-linear-my-issues-sections
The file holds every open issue of the teams, so it is not the place to see what is yours. @samp{M-x org-linear-my-issues} is: it opens an agenda of your open issues, with each one's project (in a column as wide as the longest project name) and due month and day, grouped into the sections of @code{org-linear-my-issues-sections}. By default those are triage, in progress, waiting and to do; a section whose keyword the file does not define is left out, and backlog issues are not shown. Each call rebuilds the list, even with @code{org-agenda-sticky} on. From the list, @samp{t} changes an issue's status and rebuilds the list, so the issue moves to its new section and point lands on the next one. @samp{TAB} shows an issue's heading in another window, @samp{C-x C-s} saves the file, which sends your changes to Linear, and @samp{g} refreshes the list. Issues scheduled for a later day stay out of it until that day.

@vindex org-linear-my-issues-sync
The list opens at once from the file and then syncs, unless the file has unsaved edits, and it is rebuilt with point on the same issue if the sync brings changes. Set @code{org-linear-my-issues-sync} to @samp{nil} to turn this off.

Add @code{org-linear-file} to @code{org-agenda-files} to see your issues' deadlines with the rest of your tasks.

@findex org-linear-agenda-skip
@vindex org-linear-hide-others-in-agenda
@vindex org-linear-agenda-keep-tags
@code{org-linear-agenda-skip} hides issues assigned to someone else. Set it as @code{org-agenda-skip-function-global} to apply it to every agenda view; issues with no assignee and issues assigned to you stay. So do issues with a tag in @code{org-linear-agenda-keep-tags}, @samp{nil} by default, which suits a team that labels work it shares, for instance with @samp{joint}. @code{org-linear-hide-others-in-agenda}, @samp{t} by default, turns the filter off without removing it.

@lisp
(setq org-agenda-skip-function-global #'org-linear-agenda-skip)
@end lisp

@node Transient menu
@chapter Transient menu

@findex org-linear-menu
@samp{M-x org-linear-menu} gathers the package's commands in a transient menu. The package binds it to no key, so bind it yourself if you use it often.

@multitable {aaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@tab What it does
@item @samp{m}
@tab @code{org-linear-my-issues}
@tab Show your open issues
@item @samp{v}
@tab @code{org-linear-visit}
@tab Visit the file
@item @samp{o}
@tab @code{org-linear-browse}
@tab Open the issue at point in Linear
@item @samp{s}
@tab @code{org-linear-sync}
@tab Sync now
@item @samp{a}
@tab @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode}
@tab Turn syncing on save, visit and timer on/off
@item @samp{l}
@tab @code{org-linear-show-log}
@tab Show the sync log
@end multitable

The @samp{o} key appears only when point is on an issue in the file. The @samp{a} key shows whether the mode is on, and the menu stays open after you press it.

