@node Teams and file
@section Teams and file

@vindex org-linear-teams
The user option @code{org-linear-teams} lists the keys of the teams whose issues the file mirrors, such as @samp{"ENG"}. It is @samp{nil} by default, and a sync refuses to run until you set it.

@vindex org-linear-file
@code{org-linear-file} is the mirrored Org file, by default @samp{linear.org} in @code{org-directory}.

@vindex org-linear-file-header
@code{org-linear-file-header} is the text a new file starts with. Its @samp{#+TODO} line must cover the keywords of @ref{Workflow states and keywords}, and its @samp{#+PRIORITIES} line the cookies of @code{org-linear-priorities}. Notes must go into a drawer, or they would count as part of the description: the default header's @samp{logdrawer} startup option sees to that, and you may drop it if your @code{org-log-into-drawer} is already set. Change the header before the first sync; afterwards, edit the file's header directly.

@vindex org-linear-state-file
@code{org-linear-state-file} holds each issue as it was at the last sync, by default @samp{org-linear/state.eld} under @samp{$XDG_STATE_HOME} or @samp{~/.local/state}. Deleting it makes the next sync treat Linear's values as the starting point.

@vindex org-linear-closed-retention-days
@code{org-linear-closed-retention-days}, 7 by default, is how many days a closed issue stays in the file.

@vindex org-linear-pull-interval
@code{org-linear-pull-interval}, 600 by default, is the number of seconds between background syncs in @code{org-linear-auto-sync-mode}.

@node The API key
@section The API key

The sync reads your Linear API key at the start of each sync and forgets it at the end.

@vindex org-linear-api-key-command
@code{org-linear-api-key-command}, a command as a list of strings, prints the key. It runs asynchronously, so it suits a password manager's command line. It takes precedence over the next option.

@vindex org-linear-api-key
@code{org-linear-api-key} is the key itself or a function that returns it. When it is @samp{nil}, the default, the key is read with @samp{auth-source} for the host @samp{api.linear.app}, for instance from a line in @samp{~/.authinfo.gpg}:

@example
machine api.linear.app password lin_api_YOURKEY
@end example

@vindex org-linear-api-key-forget-command
If your command caches the key, set @code{org-linear-api-key-forget-command} to a command that drops the cached copy. When Linear rejects the key, the sync runs it and retries once, so a rotated key is read afresh.

The key reaches @samp{curl} on its standard input, never on its command line, where other processes could see it.

@node Mappings
@section Mappings

@vindex org-linear-type-keywords
@vindex org-linear-state-keywords
@vindex org-linear-keyword-states
@code{org-linear-type-keywords} maps state types to keywords, @code{org-linear-state-keywords} maps individual state names to keywords, and @code{org-linear-keyword-states} chooses which state a keyword is sent as (@ref{Workflow states and keywords}). For example, a team with a ``Waiting'' state and a ``Rejected'' state might use:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-state-keywords '(("Waiting" . "WAITING"))
      org-linear-keyword-states '(("CANCELLED" . "Rejected")))
@end lisp

@vindex org-linear-priorities
@code{org-linear-priorities} maps Linear's priorities, 1 (urgent) to 4 (low), to Org cookies; it is @samp{A} to @samp{D} by default. Linear's ``no priority'' is no cookie. A cookie not listed is sent as the priority of the nearest listed cookie above it, so with numeric priorities you can map only some numbers:

@lisp
(setq org-linear-priorities '((1 . "1") (2 . "2") (3 . "4") (4 . "6")))
@end lisp

@vindex org-linear-user-tags
@code{org-linear-user-tags} maps team members' names to tags. A member not listed gets the first word of their name in lower case. Members of other teams have no tag; an issue assigned to one of them keeps that assignee, since the file cannot represent it.

@node Programs
@section Programs

@vindex org-linear-curl-program
@vindex org-linear-pandoc-program
@vindex org-linear-timeout
@code{org-linear-curl-program} and @code{org-linear-pandoc-program} name the @samp{curl} and @samp{pandoc} executables. @code{org-linear-timeout}, 60 by default, is how many seconds a request to Linear may take before it is abandoned and the sync fails.

@node Limits
@chapter Limits

@itemize
@item
Editing or deleting a note that is already in Linear is not sent; only new notes are.
@item
Comment replies are not shown.
@item
An issue that re-enters the file, for instance after being reopened, gets its comments again from Linear, but your old notes on it are not restored.
@item
Markdown headings in a description become bold lines, since an Org heading inside an issue's body would split the issue.
@item
The sync reads up to 250 labels and 250 projects, and up to 100 comments per issue.
@end itemize

